# International Trade and the Balance of Payments

> Nations trade because opportunity costs differ; the balance of
> payments records every rupee that crosses the border.

[definition]
The **balance of payments (BoP)** is a systematic record of all
economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of
the world over a period — goods, services, incomes, transfers, and
capital flows.
[/definition]

## 1. Why Trade: Two Classic Arguments

- **Absolute advantage (Adam Smith)** — a country should specialise
  in what it produces with fewer inputs than others, and trade for
  the rest.
- **Comparative advantage (David Ricardo)** — even a country better
  at *everything* gains by specialising where its **opportunity
  cost** is lowest, i.e., in goods it produces relatively (not
  absolutely) most efficiently.

[example title="Two countries, two goods"]
Hours needed per unit — India and the USA:

| | Tea (1 kg) | Software (1 unit) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| India | 10 | 30 |
| USA | 20 | 40 |

India is absolutely better at both — yet the gains from trade are
real. India's opportunity cost of tea is 1/3 software unit versus
USA's 1/2; the USA's opportunity cost of software is 2 kg tea versus
India's 3 kg. So India exports tea, the USA exports software, and
both consume beyond their own production frontiers.
[/example]

## 2. Structure of the Balance of Payments

**Current account** — flows of income and current spending:

- Merchandise trade (**balance of trade, BoT** — goods only)
- **Invisibles** — services (software exports, tourism), income
  (interest, profits), and transfers (workers' remittances)

**Capital account** — flows that change assets and liabilities:
foreign investment, loans, banking capital, and reserves. In
BPM-6 language most of these sit in the "financial account"; the
capital account proper is small. Since 1991 India's BoP has been
sustainable — current account deficits financed by capital inflows.

@diagram tree id="bop-structure" layout="tree" aria="Balance of payments structure"
  "Balance of Payments" -> "Current Account"
  "Balance of Payments" -> "Capital Account"
  "Current Account" -> "Trade in goods (BoT)"
  "Current Account" -> "Invisibles: services, income, transfers"
  "Capital Account" -> "Investment, loans, reserves"
@/diagram

## 3. Exchange Rates

| Regime | Who sets the rate | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fixed | Government/Central bank pegs value | Bretton Woods era dollar pegs |
| Flexible (floating) | Market demand and supply | US dollar, yen broadly |
| Managed float | Market-led, with central bank smoothing | **India since 1991** |

**Depreciation** (market-driven fall) and **devaluation** (policy
decision to lower a peg) both make exports cheaper abroad and
imports costlier at home — helping exporters, hurting importers of
oil and machinery, and feeding imported inflation.

## 4. Trade Policy Tools

- **Tariff** — a customs duty on imports; raises revenue and prices
  foreign goods, inviting retaliation.
- **Quota** — a physical limit on import quantity; certain shortage
  gains for protected producers, certain loss for consumers.
- **Subsidy** — grants to domestic producers; an export subsidy acts
  like a hidden tariff against rivals.

## 5. The WTO

The **World Trade Organization**, operational from **1 January 1995**,
replaced the provisional **GATT (1947)**. Both are headquartered in
Geneva.

| Basis | GATT (1947) | WTO (1995) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Coverage | Goods only | Goods, services (GATS), IP (TRIPS) |
| Legal status | Provisional agreement | Permanent organisation |
| Dispute settlement | Slow, consensus to enforce | Binding Dispute Settlement Body |
| Agreements | Narrow | Umbrella with single-package commitments |

Core principles: non-discrimination (**MFN** — any concession to one
member extends to all), national treatment, and tariff bindings.
India is a founding member.

## 6. India's Trade Profile

Durable facts: India consistently runs a **merchandise trade
deficit**, importing crude oil, gold, and electronics while
exporting petroleum products, gems and jewellery, pharma, textiles,
and engineering goods. Services — led by software — and remittances
comfortably offset the goods gap in most years. **FDI** enters
through the automatic route (no approval needed for most sectors)
and the government (approval) route for sensitive ones.

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | One-line memory |
| --- | --- |
| Comparative advantage | Specialise where opportunity cost is lowest |
| BoT | Goods exports minus goods imports |
| Invisibles | Services, income, transfers |
| Current vs capital account | Current spending vs asset-changing flows |
| Depreciation vs devaluation | Market fall vs policy cut |
| Rupee falls | Exports gain, imports and inflation hurt |
| GATT → WTO | 1947 provisional → 1995 permanent, wider, binding |

[summary]
Trade follows comparative, not absolute, advantage; the BoP books it
all as current flows (goods plus invisibles) and capital flows,
settled through exchange rates — managed in India's case since 1991.
Tariffs, quotas, and subsidies protect at a price, and the WTO has
policed that price since it replaced GATT in 1995.
[/summary]
